he name UNKNOWN is already injected into your program's namespace (use the bare name UNKNOWN \u2014 do not import or define it). render(state) MAY place UNKNOWN in any cell the program cannot model; the validator SKIPS every UNKNOWN cell, so an unmodelable region never fails validation. If a board region changes after every action regardless of which action (e.g. an energy/timer/progress bar), either model it EXACTLY or mark those cells UNKNOWN \u2014 do NOT assume it stays constant.\nOBJECTS: segment(grid) is already injected into your namespace (bare name \u2014 do not import or define it). It returns {nodes, adjacency_list}; each node has color, pixels, boundary, and children. PREFER expressing mechanics relative to objects rather than absolute cell indices: e.g. locate the avatar by its color with segment(grid) each step and move it (dr, dc), instead of hardcoding a row/col \u2014 absolute indices break on new levels where the same object sits elsewhere. Purely cellular games (no discrete movable objects) may still use direct grid logic.\n",
  "raw_llm_response": "Based on the visual evidence:\n- **Action:** The player moves down one step.\n- **Effect:** The orange pixel (color 12) below the player is replaced by a grey pixel (color 5). This indicates that the action \"moves\" or \"paints\" the path behind the player with the background color, effectively erasing the previous position's marker.\n- **State:** The player's position changes from `(r, c)` to `(r+1, c)`.\n\nThe world model tracks the player's position and updates it upon movement actions. The \"path\" is implicitly handled by the rendering logic which paints the background color at the old location (or simply doesn't paint the marker there). Since the grid shows the marker disappearing, we can model this as:\n1.  Update player position.\n2.  Render the new player position with its specific marker (orange pixel below head).\n3.  The rest of the grid remains static (background).\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\n# Assuming UNKNOWN is provided in scope\n# Assuming segment is provided in scope\n\ndef init_state(grid):\n    # Find the player. Based on previous frames, the player is a distinct object.\n    # In this specific frame, we see a character with color 7 (cyan) and an orange pixel below it.\n    # We can identify the player by finding the unique pattern or just scanning for the head.\n    # Let's find the bounding box of the cyan (7) pixels to locate the player.\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows > 0 else 0\n    \n    player_pos = None\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] == 7:\n                # Found the head. Check if it has an orange pixel below to confirm it's the active player avatar\n                if r + 1 < rows and grid[r+1][c] == 12:\n                    player_pos = (r, c)\n                    break\n        if player_pos:\n            break\n            \n    # If we can't find the specific pattern, fallback to finding any cyan pixel\n    if player_pos is None:\n        for r in range(rows):\n            for c in range(cols):\n                if grid[r][c] == 7:\n                    player_pos = (r, c)\n                    break\n            if player_pos:\n                break\n\n    # Identify background color. The large area of grey is likely the background.\n    # In the diff, the orange pixel turns to grey (5). So 5 is the \"erased\" color.\n    # Let's assume the background is the most frequent color or the one surrounding the objects.\n    # From the grid data provided in previous steps, 4 and 5 are common backgrounds.\n    # In this specific crop, the background seems to be grey (5).\n    \n    state = {\n        \"player_pos\": player_pos,\n        \"grid_dims\": (rows, cols),\n        \"background_color\": 5 # Based on the diff turning orange to grey\n    }\n    return state\n\ndef step(state, action):\n    new_state = copy.deepcopy(state)\n    events = []\n    \n    r, c = state[\"player_pos\"]\n    \n    if action == \"ACTION1\": # Move Up\n        new_state[\"player_pos\"] = (r - 1, c)\n        events.append(\"move_up\")\n    elif action == \"ACTION2\": # Move Down\n        new_state[\"player_pos\"] = (r + 1, c)\n        events.append(\"move_down\")\n    elif action == \"ACTION3\": # Move Left\n        new_state[\"player_pos\"] = (r, c - 1)\n        events.append(\"move_left\")\n    elif action == \"ACTION4\": # Move Right\n        new_state[\"player_pos\"] = (r, c + 1)\n        events.append(\"move_right\")\n        \n    return new_state, events\n\ndef render(state):\n    rows, cols = state[\"grid_dims\"]\n    grid = [[state[\"background_color\"] for _ in range(cols)] for _ in range(rows)]\n    \n    pr, pc = state[\"player_pos\"]\n    \n    # Draw the player head (cyan 7)\n    if 0 <= pr < rows and 0 <= pc < cols:\n        grid[pr][pc] = 7\n        \n    # Draw the marker below the player (orange 12)\n    # The diff shows the orange pixel is part of the player object's footprint\n    if 0 <= pr + 1 < rows and 0 <= pc < cols:\n        grid[pr+1][pc] = 12\n        \n    return grid\n\ndef is_win(state):\n    # No win condition observed yet.\n    return False\n\ndef legal_actions(state):\n    return [\"ACTION1\", \"ACTION2\", \"ACTION3\", \"ACTION4\"]\n```",
